# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: error-processing-per-dialect -- how *error*, exit, catch-all and failing builtins differ between AutoCAD and BricsCAD
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: divergence-cat
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-11-03

* Why

A client application (SCHMS+, railway schematics) found that some errors
were silent under BricsCAD and visible under AutoCAD. The question was
whether the error MECHANISM differs (when and where *error* runs, what it
sees), or whether some FUNCTIONS fail differently. This ticket collects the
measurements made to answer it on 2026-11-03, and lists every divergence the
probes found, so that clautolisp can model each one per dialect.

Products measured:

| label           | product                                    | how                                 |
|-----------------+--------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------|
| AutoCAD-2022    | AutoCAD 2022 FR, accoreconsole (no COM)    | /i empty.dwg /s run.scr, Windows 11 |
| BricsCAD-V25    | BricsCAD V25.2.05 fr_FR, Windows           | /Automation /b run.scr              |
| BricsCAD-V26    | BricsCAD V26.2.7 fr_FR, macOS (Rosetta)    | -B run.scr                          |
| clautolisp-*    | clautolisp 2.3.81, --dialect autocad / bricscad | -x, same probe files           |

AutoCAD GUI (acad.exe) was NOT measured. accoreconsole has no COM, so its
vla-* rows say nothing about the GUI.

* The probes

All under =probes/error-processing/= (README.org there):

- =probe-error-handler.lsp=: C:P1 .. C:P12. One command per situation, run
  at TOP LEVEL from the script (NOT under vl-catch-all-apply, which is why
  alfe cannot measure this, see local-error-handler-not-called.issue). Each
  handler writes what it sees to probe-log.txt.
- =probe-error-catalogue.lsp=: C:PCAT. 546 expressions held as STRINGS, READ
  then EVAL under vl-catch-all-apply, one at a time. For each one the probe
  logs VALUE or ERROR+message, and ERRNO. A BEGIN line before each case
  names the case that killed the engine; the driver then restarts after it.
- =run-bricscad-macos.sh=, =run-windows.sh=, =drive-catalogue.sh=: launch,
  crash-resume. =summarize.py= builds the vendor tables below from the logs,
  and =gaps.py= the clautolisp-against-vendor tables of 3.
- =results/=: every raw log, plus catalogue-comparison.org (all 546 cases,
  5 columns) and catalogue-divergences.org (vendor divergences, classified).

Caveat on the catalogue: a case evaluated through READ + EVAL is not always
the same as the form written in a loaded file. See 2.3 on LAMBDA.

* 1. The *error* mechanism (probe-error-handler.lsp)

| case | situation                                                                  | AutoCAD-2022                                                      | BricsCAD V25 = V26                                  |
|------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------|
| P1   | *error* setq'd inside a function, *push-error-using-stack*, (/ 1 0) in the thunk | called, "division par zéro"; sees the function's locals; restored | called, "divide by zero at [/]"; same              |
| P2   | same without *push-error-using-stack*                                      | same: locals visible                                              | same                                                |
| P3   | local *error* (defun f (/ *error*) ...) in a SUB-function                  | called, sees f's locals                                           | same                                                |
| P4   | local *error* in the command, error 2 calls deeper                         | called, sees the DEEPER function's locals too                    | same                                                |
| P5   | error under vl-catch-all-apply                                             | *error* not called                                                | same                                                |
| P6   | error raised INSIDE *error*                                                | handler aborted; the 2nd error's message printed                  | IGNORED: the handler runs to its end                |
| P8   | (c:inner) called from c:outer, error in inner                              | outer's local handler                                             | same                                                |
| P9   | (exit)                                                                     | *error* "quitter / sortir abandon"                                | "quit / exit abort"                                 |
| P10  | (vl-exit-with-error "x") in a command, no VLX                              | *error* NOT called, command ends silently                         | same                                                |
| P11  | (command "_LINE" "pas-un-point" "")                                        | *error* "Fonction annulée" (the ESC message); the SCRIPT is cancelled too | no error; command returns nil; CMDACTIVE 4 (script only); execution goes on |
| P12  | (error "x")                                                                | "no function definition: ERROR"                                   | *error* receives "error": the message is LOST       |
| P1F  | thunk passed as (function (lambda ...)), called as (f)                     | "fonction incorrecte: (quote #<SUBR @… -lambda->)"                | works                                               |

Conclusions:

- *error* runs BEFORE unwinding on BOTH products: every dynamic binding of
  the failing extent is still visible. The belief that BricsCAD unwinds first
  came from its truncated vl-bt only. clautolisp's 2.3.28 fix
  (local-error-handler-not-called.issue: HANDLER-BIND, handler at signal
  time) matches both vendors. The "Vendor evidence: not measurable" note
  there can now cite P3/P4.
- To model per dialect: P6 (error inside *error*), P11 (command refusing
  input: error vs nil, and script cancellation on AutoCAD), P12 (BricsCAD's
  ERROR gives *error* the bare word "error"), P1F (calling a
  (function (lambda)) value bound to a variable, AutoCAD interpreted code).
- P10: vl-exit-with-error outside a VLX never reaches *error*, on either
  product. This complements vl-exit-outside-vlx-caught-diverges.issue,
  which measured only the vl-catch-all-apply side.

* 2. Catalogue: where AutoCAD and BricsCAD differ

summarize.py over the raw logs: 546 cases, 368 differ between AutoCAD-2022
and BricsCAD-V26. 258 of these differ in the message only (both signal), 45
return different values, and 65 differ in KIND (one signals, returns nil, a
value, or dies). BricsCAD V25 Windows and V26 macOS give the same KIND of
answer everywhere except the crash of 2.1. Their values differ for the
encoding cases (2.7) and for textbox, and their messages differ for setvar
and vla-* errors.
Full tables: probes/error-processing/results/catalogue-divergences.org
(classified) and catalogue-comparison.org (every case, five columns).

** 2.1 Crashes (the engine dies; vl-catch-all-apply does not help)

| # | expression | AutoCAD-2022 | BricsCAD-V25 | BricsCAD-V26 |
|---+---+---+---+---|
| 157 | =(reverse '(1 . 2))= | error "liste incorrecte: 2" | error, then lispex.dll ACCESS_VIOLATION a few evaluations later | SIGSEGV at once (EXC_BAD_ACCESS 0x40) |
| 392 | =(command-s "_LINE" "pas-un-point" "")= | ACCESS_VIOLATION 0xC0000005 ("fonction unwind ignorée exception"); also at top level (P13: segfault) | nil | nil |
| 397 | =(command-s "_LAYER" "_S" "PCAT_NOPE_LAYER" "")= | dies | nil | nil |
| 399 | =(command-s "_CIRCLE" '(0 0) -5)= | dies | nil | nil |

- 157 on V25: the run where it was skipped, and the 11 bisection runs
  without it, never crashed. The two runs that contained it crashed in lispex.dll
  at the same address (reportMemory+0x3CEC), during a LATER Lisp
  evaluation. Crash reports: results/raw/crash-bricscad-*.
- 392/397/399: each case alone reproduces it, so it does not depend on
  case order. P13 (no catch-all, at top level) segfaults the process; P14
  (vl-catch-all-apply 'command-s ...) ends in the access violation. Console:
  results/raw/console-autocad-2022-command-s-refused-input-case-392.txt.
  Measured on accoreconsole only; acad.exe not measured.

